  "id": "DrugBank_DB04819 - MONDO_grouped_11786_5324_24332",
  "verbalization": "Methapyrilene is implicated in the pathogenesis of allergic rhinitis through a series of indirect pathways. The drug is associated with Angioedema and Vasomotor Rhinitis, both of which are known to contribute to allergic rhinitis. Angioedema, in particular, is linked to Rupatadine and Bilastine, which are antihistamine agents that inhibit histamine release and reduce inflammation. These mechanisms are consistent with the known pathophysiology of allergic rhinitis, where histamine-mediated inflammation plays a central role. The evidence suggests that Methapyrilene may exert a modulatory effect on inflammatory pathways, potentially contributing to the development or exacerbation of allergic rhinitis. However, the direct mechanism by which Methapyrilene influences allergic rhinitis remains unclear, and further studies are needed to elucidate its precise role. The integration of these pathways highlights the importance of considering pharmacological interactions and their downstream effects in the context of allergic rhinitis.",
